window.OS_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"C for Systems: Pointers & Memory","f":"p1-c-for-systems.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After int x = 5; int *p = &x; *p = 7; , what is the value of x ?","o":["5, because p is a copy","7, because writing through p changes the int at x's address","The address of x","It doesn't compile"],"a":1,"w":"p holds the address of x , so *p = 7 writes 7 into x 's four bytes."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"C for Systems: Pointers & Memory","f":"p1-c-for-systems.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does read(fd, buf, n) take a pointer to a buffer instead of returning the data?","o":["Pointers are faster than return values","The kernel cannot return integers","C passes arguments by value, so to fill memory the caller owns, the kernel needs that memory's address","So that read() can call free() on it"],"a":2,"w":"The caller owns the buffer and passes its address; the kernel copies file data into it and returns only the byte count (or −1 with errno )."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"C for Systems: Pointers & Memory","f":"p1-c-for-systems.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A function declares char buf[64]; , fills it and returns buf . What goes wrong?","o":["buf lives in the function's stack frame, which is gone after return, so the caller gets a dangling pointer","Nothing: arrays are always on the heap","It leaks 64 bytes","It is a double free"],"a":0,"w":"Locals die with their frame. The next function call reuses those bytes, so the caller's \"string\" changes under it. Return heap memory (and document who frees it) or let the caller pass a buffer in."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"C for Systems: Pointers & Memory","f":"p1-c-for-systems.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is sizeof p when char *p = malloc(1000); on x86-64 Linux?","o":["1000","1","4","8: it is the size of the pointer itself, not the block it points to"],"a":3,"w":"A pointer is an 8-byte address on a 64-bit machine. C does not remember the size of the block; you must carry the length yourself."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"C for Systems: Pointers & Memory","f":"p1-c-for-systems.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A program frees a block and later writes to it through the old pointer. Why is this so dangerous?","o":["It always crashes immediately","The allocator may have handed the block to other code, so the write silently corrupts unrelated data","The kernel deletes the process's heap","It only leaks memory"],"a":1,"w":"Use-after-free rarely crashes at once; it corrupts whatever now lives in the block, often far from the bug, and attackers can arrange for useful objects to land there."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Structs, Arrays & Strings in C","f":"p2-structs-arrays-strings.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On x86-64, what is sizeof(struct { char a; double d; char b; }) ?","o":["10","16","24","17"],"a":2,"w":"a at 0, 7 bytes padding, d at 8–15, b at 16, then 7 bytes of tail padding to a multiple of 8: 24. Putting d first gives 16."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Structs, Arrays & Strings in C","f":"p2-structs-arrays-strings.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With uint32_t *p holding address 0x1000, what address does p + 3 hold?","o":["0x100C: pointer arithmetic moves by 3 × sizeof(uint32_t) = 12 bytes","0x1003","0x1018","It depends on the compiler"],"a":0,"w":"Adding n to a pointer advances it by n elements, each 4 bytes here: 0x1000 + 12 = 0x100C."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Structs, Arrays & Strings in C","f":"p2-structs-arrays-strings.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Inside void f(char buf[64]) , what is sizeof buf ?","o":["64","65","1","8: an array parameter is really a pointer"],"a":3,"w":"Array parameters decay to pointers; the 64 is ignored. That's why C functions take an explicit length."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Structs, Arrays & Strings in C","f":"p2-structs-arrays-strings.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What made Heartbleed possible?","o":["A weak encryption algorithm","Copying as many bytes as a length field in the request claimed, without checking it against the real request size","A stack overflow in a recursive function","A race condition between two threads"],"a":1,"w":"The heartbeat handler trusted the peer's length field and copied up to 64 KiB of adjacent heap memory into the reply. One bounds check fixed it."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Structs, Arrays & Strings in C","f":"p2-structs-arrays-strings.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does the kernel's read() system call reach the right code for a pipe versus an ext4 file?","o":["A long switch statement on the file type","Separate system calls for each file type","It calls through the function pointer in the file's file_operations table, set when the file was opened","The C library decides before entering the kernel"],"a":2,"w":"f->f_op->read(...) is an indirect call through a struct of function pointers: the kernel's way of expressing an interface in C."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"How a CPU Runs Your Code","f":"p3-how-cpu-runs-code.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does the x86-64 call instruction do?","o":["Jumps to the function and saves all registers","Pushes the address of the next instruction onto the stack, then sets rip to the function's address","Creates a new thread for the function","Asks the kernel to run the function"],"a":1,"w":"call pushes the return address (rsp −= 8) and jumps. ret pops it back into rip . Saving other registers is the compiled function's job."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"How a CPU Runs Your Code","f":"p3-how-cpu-runs-code.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On Linux x86-64, where does a function find its first integer argument?","o":["On the stack at rsp","In rax","In rip","In the rdi register"],"a":3,"w":"The System V calling convention passes the first six integer or pointer arguments in rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8 and r9; the return value comes back in rax."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"How a CPU Runs Your Code","f":"p3-how-cpu-runs-code.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What must the kernel save so that a paused thread can later continue exactly where it stopped?","o":["Its registers, including rip and rsp","A copy of its whole stack","Its source code line number","Nothing; threads restart from the beginning"],"a":0,"w":"The stack stays in memory where it is. The register values, especially the program counter and stack pointer, are what define \"where it was\", and a context switch saves and restores them."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"How a CPU Runs Your Code","f":"p3-how-cpu-runs-code.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Roughly what does a mispredicted branch cost on a modern x86 core?","o":["Nothing; prediction is free","About 1 cycle","About 15–20 cycles of discarded pipeline work","About a millisecond"],"a":2,"w":"The pipeline has to be flushed and refilled from the correct path. That's cheap in absolute terms but adds up quickly in a hot loop."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"How a CPU Runs Your Code","f":"p3-how-cpu-runs-code.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How did operating systems mitigate Meltdown?","o":["By disabling the CPU cache","With kernel page-table isolation: unmapping the kernel from user page tables, so syscalls and interrupts switch page tables","By removing system calls","By running all programs in kernel mode"],"a":1,"w":"KPTI keeps kernel memory out of the address space while user code runs, so speculative reads have nothing to read, at the cost of extra work on every kernel entry and exit."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"The Memory Hierarchy","f":"p4-memory-hierarchy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Roughly how long does a read from main memory (DRAM) take when it misses every cache?","o":["About 1 ns","About 10 µs","About 100 ns","About 10 ms"],"a":2,"w":"Around 80–100 ns: a hundred times an L1 hit, and hundreds of simple instructions' worth of time."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"The Memory Hierarchy","f":"p4-memory-hierarchy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Your code reads one 4-byte int that is not in any cache. How much data moves from DRAM into the cache?","o":["A whole 64-byte cache line","Exactly 4 bytes","A 4 KiB page","The whole array"],"a":0,"w":"Caches transfer whole lines, 64 bytes on x86-64. The neighbouring 60 bytes come along, which is why spatial locality pays."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"The Memory Hierarchy","f":"p4-memory-hierarchy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which access pattern makes the best use of the cache for a large C array int m[R][C] ?","o":["Outer loop over columns, inner loop over rows","Random order, to spread the load","It makes no difference","Outer loop over rows, inner loop over columns"],"a":3,"w":"C is row-major, so m[r][c] and m[r][c+1] are adjacent. Varying the last index fastest walks memory in order."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"The Memory Hierarchy","f":"p4-memory-hierarchy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An NVMe SSD streams 5 GB/s, yet one random 4 KiB read takes ~80 µs. How can both be true?","o":["The 5 GB/s figure is false advertising","Throughput comes from many requests in flight at once; latency is the time for one","Only reads from the page cache reach 5 GB/s","The SSD is faster for random reads than sequential ones"],"a":1,"w":"Latency and throughput are different measures. With dozens of overlapping requests, each still taking ~80 µs, the device moves gigabytes per second."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"The Memory Hierarchy","f":"p4-memory-hierarchy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does Kafka rely on the OS page cache and sequential disk writes instead of a big in-process cache?","o":["Java cannot allocate large arrays","Disks are faster than RAM","Sequential I/O is fast at every level, and the kernel already caches recent file data in RAM, so a second cache would duplicate it","The page cache is persistent across power loss"],"a":2,"w":"Kafka's design leans on access patterns: append and read logs in order, and let the kernel's page cache hold the hot tail. The page cache is not durable; that's what fsync is for (Chapter 31)."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Bits, Hex & Binary Data","f":"p5-bits-hex-binary.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is 0xff in decimal?","o":["256","255","15","127"],"a":1,"w":"15 × 16 + 15 = 255: eight bits, all set."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Bits, Hex & Binary Data","f":"p5-bits-hex-binary.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How do you turn on O_NONBLOCK in an existing flags value without changing the other flags?","o":["flags = O_NONBLOCK;","flags &= O_NONBLOCK;","flags ^= ~O_NONBLOCK;","flags |= O_NONBLOCK;"],"a":3,"w":"OR sets the bit and leaves every other bit alone. Plain assignment wipes the other flags; AND would clear them."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Bits, Hex & Binary Data","f":"p5-bits-hex-binary.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With 4 KiB pages, what is the offset within the page of address 0x00403a7c ?","o":["0xa7c","0x403","0x7c","0x3a7c"],"a":0,"w":"4 KiB = 2 12 , so the offset is the low 12 bits: the last three hex digits, 0xa7c . The page number is 0x403 ."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Bits, Hex & Binary Data","f":"p5-bits-hex-binary.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On an x86 machine, how is the 32-bit value 0x12345678 laid out in memory, lowest address first?","o":["12 34 56 78","21 43 65 87","78 56 34 12","It depends on the compiler"],"a":2,"w":"x86 is little-endian: the least significant byte, 0x78 , comes first. Network byte order is the reverse, which is why htonl exists."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Bits, Hex & Binary Data","f":"p5-bits-hex-binary.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did the Boeing 787's generator controllers need a reboot at least every 248 days?","o":["Their memory leaked","A signed 32-bit counter of hundredths of a second would overflow after about 2 31 ÷ 100 seconds","Their clocks drifted","The disk filled up with logs"],"a":1,"w":"2,147,483,648 hundredths of a second is about 248.6 days. Integer width is a design decision with an expiry date."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Compiling & Linking","f":"p6-compiling-linking.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which stage turns #include <stdio.h> into actual declarations?","o":["The linker","The assembler","The preprocessor","The dynamic loader"],"a":2,"w":"The preprocessor pastes the header's text in; gcc -E shows the result."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Compiling & Linking","f":"p6-compiling-linking.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"nm main.o shows U clamp . What does that mean?","o":["main.o uses clamp but doesn't define it; the linker must find it elsewhere","clamp is unused","clamp is defined in main.o's text section","clamp is a static function"],"a":0,"w":"U means undefined in this object file: a relocation the linker will fill in with clamp's address."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Compiling & Linking","f":"p6-compiling-linking.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A program declares static char buf[1 << 20]; at file scope. How much does it add to the executable file's size?","o":["1 MiB","2 MiB","It depends on the contents","Almost nothing: it's in .bss, which records only a size; the kernel provides zeroed pages at run time"],"a":3,"w":"Zero-initialised globals go in .bss , which takes no file space. Only non-zero initial values need to be stored."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Compiling & Linking","f":"p6-compiling-linking.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A program built on one machine fails on another with \"error while loading shared libraries: libssl.so.3: cannot open shared object file\". When did this fail?","o":["At compile time","At start-up, when the dynamic loader tried to map a library the target machine doesn't have","At link time on the build machine","Inside main(), at the first call to SSL"],"a":1,"w":"The executable records that it needs libssl.so.3; the loader looks for it before main runs. Install the library or link it statically."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Compiling & Linking","f":"p6-compiling-linking.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does dynamic linking save RAM on a server running 200 processes that all use libc?","o":["Dynamically linked code runs faster","libc is loaded into the kernel","libc.so's code pages are mapped read-only from the same file, so all 200 processes share one copy in physical memory","Only one process can use libc at a time"],"a":2,"w":"Read-only file mappings are backed by the same page-cache pages in every process (Chapter 30). A static libc would be a separate copy inside each binary."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"The Linux Shell & Files","f":"p7-linux-shell-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does -rwxr-x--- mean for a file?","o":["Everyone can read it; only the owner can run it","Owner: read, write, execute; group: read and execute; others: nothing","It's a directory anyone can enter","Owner: read only; group: everything"],"a":1,"w":"After the type character come three triples: owner rwx , group r-x , others --- . In octal, 0750."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"The Linux Shell & Files","f":"p7-linux-shell-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which command captures both normal output and error messages of backup.sh into run.log ?","o":["./backup.sh 2>&1 > run.log","./backup.sh | run.log","./backup.sh > run.log 1>&2","./backup.sh > run.log 2>&1"],"a":3,"w":"Redirections apply left to right: first stdout goes to the file, then stderr is pointed at wherever stdout now points. The reverse order sends stderr to the terminal."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"The Linux Shell & Files","f":"p7-linux-shell-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A server ignores kill 4120 . What does kill -9 4120 do differently?","o":["Sends SIGKILL, which the process cannot catch or ignore, so the kernel terminates it without clean-up","Sends SIGTERM nine times","Kills the process and all its parents","Restarts the process"],"a":0,"w":"kill defaults to SIGTERM (15), which programs may handle or ignore. SIGKILL (9) is enforced by the kernel."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"The Linux Shell & Files","f":"p7-linux-shell-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You run export API_KEY=abc in a terminal. Which processes see the new variable?","o":["Every process on the machine","The shell's parent process","Programs started from this shell afterwards, which receive a copy of its environment","Programs already running that were started from this shell"],"a":2,"w":"The environment is copied into a child when it's created. Existing processes keep the copy they got at their start."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"The Linux Shell & Files","f":"p7-linux-shell-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In ps aux | grep nginx , how is ps 's output delivered to grep ?","o":["ps writes a temporary file that grep opens when ps finishes","Through a kernel pipe: ps's stdout is the write end, grep's stdin the read end, and both run at the same time","The shell collects ps's output and types it into grep","Through an environment variable"],"a":1,"w":"The shell creates a pipe, then starts both programs with their standard streams pointed at its two ends. Chapters 9, 10 and 41 show the system calls."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Reading Syscalls & Man Pages","f":"p8-syscalls-man-pages.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which manual section documents openat , and which documents fopen ?","o":["Both are in section 1","openat in section 3, fopen in section 2","openat (a system call) in section 2, fopen (a library function) in section 3","Both are in section 8"],"a":2,"w":"Section 2 is system calls, section 3 library functions. fopen calls openat internally and adds a user-space buffer."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Reading Syscalls & Man Pages","f":"p8-syscalls-man-pages.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"read(fd, buf, 4096) returns 1200. What does that mean?","o":["1200 bytes were read; this is not an error, and the caller should loop if it needs more","An error with code 1200","The file is exactly 1200 bytes long","2896 bytes were lost"],"a":0,"w":"A short read is normal, especially on pipes and sockets. Only 0 (end of file) and −1 (error) are special."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Reading Syscalls & Man Pages","f":"p8-syscalls-man-pages.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"When is the value of errno meaningful?","o":["Always: it holds the last call's result","Only when it is zero","After any successful system call","Immediately after a call that reported failure (usually by returning −1)"],"a":3,"w":"Successful calls don't reset errno , so it can hold a stale value. Check the return value first."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Reading Syscalls & Man Pages","f":"p8-syscalls-man-pages.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Your program forks worker processes, and the error happens in a worker. Which strace option do you need?","o":["-c","-f , to follow child processes and threads","-T","-e trace=file"],"a":1,"w":"Without -f , strace traces only the process it started; children created by fork or clone are invisible."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Reading Syscalls & Man Pages","f":"p8-syscalls-man-pages.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In an strace -T output, one line reads poll([{fd=3, events=POLLIN}], 1, 5000) = 0 (Timeout) <5.005112> . What happened?","o":["The program used 5 seconds of CPU","fd 3 was closed","The program waited the full 5,000 ms timeout for data on fd 3 that never came","poll failed with an error"],"a":2,"w":"The third argument is the timeout in milliseconds, a return of 0 means no fd became ready, and the time in angle brackets confirms the 5-second wait."},{"n":1,"ti":"What an Operating System Does","f":"01-what-an-os-does.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A one-core machine runs a program stuck in an infinite loop, yet the web server on the same machine keeps answering. What makes that possible?","o":["The looping program eventually yields the CPU","A timer interrupt returns control to the kernel, whose scheduler gives the CPU to other processes","The web server runs on the GPU","The MMU stops the loop"],"a":1,"w":"Preemptive scheduling doesn't need the program's cooperation: the hardware timer interrupts it and the kernel decides who runs next."},{"n":1,"ti":"What an Operating System Does","f":"01-what-an-os-does.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which hardware feature stops a bad pointer in one process from corrupting another process's memory?","o":["The CPU cache","The timer interrupt","The file system","The MMU, checking each access against the current process's page tables"],"a":3,"w":"Each process's page tables map only its own memory. An access outside it causes a fault, and the kernel typically kills the process with SIGSEGV."},{"n":1,"ti":"What an Operating System Does","f":"01-what-an-os-does.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Which of these is an OS abstraction rather than a piece of hardware?","o":["A file","A disk sector","A CPU register","A DRAM chip"],"a":0,"w":"Files are an illusion the kernel builds out of disk blocks (and sometimes nothing at all, like /proc files)."},{"n":1,"ti":"What an Operating System Does","f":"01-what-an-os-does.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In a microkernel, where does a file system usually run?","o":["Inside the kernel, like Linux","In the disk's firmware","As a user-space server process that the kernel passes messages to","In the bootloader"],"a":2,"w":"Microkernels keep only IPC, threads and address spaces privileged; file systems and drivers are ordinary processes."},{"n":1,"ti":"What an Operating System Does","f":"01-what-an-os-does.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why did the July 2024 CrowdStrike fault crash whole machines rather than one application?","o":["It deleted system files","The faulty code ran in a kernel-mode driver, where an invalid memory access has no higher layer to contain it","It filled the disk","It disabled the network"],"a":1,"w":"In user mode the same bug would have killed one process. In kernel mode, the OS itself fails, so the system stops."},{"n":2,"ti":"Kernel Mode, User Mode & System Calls","f":"02-kernel-user-mode-syscalls.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"On Linux x86-64, where does the kernel find which system call a program wants?","o":["On the user stack","In a file under /proc","In the rax register, used as an index into the system-call table","In the instruction's immediate operand"],"a":2,"w":"The number goes in rax (read = 0, write = 1); arguments in rdi, rsi, rdx, r10, r8, r9. The entry code indexes sys_call_table with it."},{"n":2,"ti":"Kernel Mode, User Mode & System Calls","f":"02-kernel-user-mode-syscalls.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A thread calls read() on a file whose data isn't in memory. What does it do while the SSD works for 80 µs?","o":["It sleeps in a wait queue; the scheduler runs other threads until the I/O-complete interrupt wakes it","It spins in kernel mode checking the disk","It returns EAGAIN immediately","The whole CPU halts"],"a":0,"w":"Blocking means sleeping, not spinning: the thread is taken off the run queue and costs no CPU until the device's interrupt marks it runnable again."},{"n":2,"ti":"Kernel Mode, User Mode & System Calls","f":"02-kernel-user-mode-syscalls.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why can't a user program simply jump to the kernel's file-reading code?","o":["The kernel's code is encrypted","It would be too slow","The kernel's functions are not in memory","Kernel pages aren't accessible in user mode, and the only way to raise privilege is through entry points the kernel registered with the CPU"],"a":3,"w":"The page tables mark kernel memory supervisor-only, and syscall always lands at the kernel's chosen entry point, so user code can request but never steer."},{"n":2,"ti":"Kernel Mode, User Mode & System Calls","f":"02-kernel-user-mode-syscalls.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A program passes an invalid pointer as read() 's buffer. What happens?","o":["The kernel crashes","read() returns −1 with errno = EFAULT","The data is written to address 0","The kernel allocates a new buffer"],"a":1,"w":"copy_to_user detects the bad address and the call fails with EFAULT. The kernel never trusts a user pointer."},{"n":2,"ti":"Kernel Mode, User Mode & System Calls","f":"02-kernel-user-mode-syscalls.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the vDSO for?","o":["Loading device drivers","Making system calls in parallel","Letting calls like clock_gettime run in user mode by reading kernel-maintained data from a page mapped into every process","Debugging the kernel"],"a":2,"w":"The kernel publishes timekeeping data on a read-only page; the vDSO code reads it in ~20 ns, avoiding a mode switch."},{"n":3,"ti":"Processes: fork, exec, wait","f":"03-processes-fork-exec-wait.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In the child process, what does fork() return?","o":["The parent's PID","0","The child's own PID","−1"],"a":1,"w":"0 in the child, the child's PID in the parent, −1 (in the parent, with no child created) on failure. The child can call getppid() for its parent's PID."},{"n":3,"ti":"Processes: fork, exec, wait","f":"03-processes-fork-exec-wait.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A process with PID 4102 calls execvp(\"python3\", argv) successfully. What is its PID afterwards?","o":["A new PID assigned by the kernel","0","Its parent's PID","Still 4102: exec replaces the program, not the process"],"a":3,"w":"exec keeps the process (PID, parent, open fds) and replaces its code and memory. Creating a new process is fork's job."},{"n":3,"ti":"Processes: fork, exec, wait","f":"03-processes-fork-exec-wait.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"How many lines does this print? for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) fork(); printf(\"x\\n\");","o":["4","2","3","1"],"a":0,"w":"Two forks give 2² = 4 processes, and each prints once."},{"n":3,"ti":"Processes: fork, exec, wait","f":"03-processes-fork-exec-wait.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"ps shows hundreds of <defunct> children of your server. What's the fix?","o":["kill -9 each defunct PID","Add more RAM","Make the server reap children, e.g. waitpid(-1, &st, WNOHANG) in a loop on SIGCHLD","Reboot weekly"],"a":2,"w":"Zombies are already dead, so signals do nothing. Only a wait() by the parent (or by PID 1 after the parent dies) removes them."},{"n":3,"ti":"Processes: fork, exec, wait","f":"03-processes-fork-exec-wait.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A container's main process exits with code 137. What most likely happened?","o":["It called exit(137)","It was killed by SIGKILL (128 + 9), very often by the out-of-memory killer","It segfaulted","It couldn't find its executable"],"a":1,"w":"Shells and container runtimes report death by signal n as 128 + n. 137 is SIGKILL; 139 is SIGSEGV; 127 is \"command not found\"."}];
